> # PIXL/1 normative specification

Status: implemented version 1.0.0. PIXL is a project-specific, agent-operated UDL; this document is its decoder 
contract. The wire form has no human usability requirement. Host profiles are a separate translating layer. Reserved 
notation cannot silently acquire a new meaning.

## Wire grammar

Input is UTF-8, maximum 16 MiB. ASCII whitespace is insignificant outside literals. There are no comments, escapes, 
implicit coercions, expressions, interpolation, executable imports, or indentation semantics. Parsing is bounded to 64 
nested value containers and 100,000 records. ASCII identifiers match `[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_.-]{0,95}`. IDs are case 
sensitive and document-unique.

```ebnf
document = "PX1", whitespace, revision, whitespace, { record, whitespace } ;
record   = "@", identifier, ":", kind, map ;
map      = "{", [ identifier, "=", value, { ";", identifier, "=", value } ], "}" ;
list     = "[", [ value, { ",", value } ], "]" ;
value    = literal | reference | symbol | integer | list | map | "+" | "-" | "?" ;
reference= "@", identifier ;
symbol   = identifier ;
integer  = [ "-" ], digit, { digit } ;
literal  = "~", unsigned-byte-count, ":", exactly-that-many-UTF8-bytes ;
kind     = one-uppercase-ASCII-letter ;
revision = unsigned-integer ;
```

`+` is true, `-` is false, `?` is absent/null. Integers are signed 64-bit; lengths and revisions are nonnegative. 
Floating-point wire values are intentionally absent: coordinates are integer pixels; duration is integer milliseconds; 
fractional quantities use declared fixed-point units in asset metadata, or opaque provider JSON at the boundary. A 
symbol and a literal with the same visible text are different values. A reference is never a string. Lengths count 
UTF-8 bytes, not characters. `~0:` is an empty literal. A literal may contain newlines, delimiters, backslashes or NUL 
without acquiring syntax. A malformed UTF-8 length boundary is an error. Duplicate fields are errors.

Canonical encoding starts `PX1 <revision>\n`, sorts records by ID and map fields by ASCII key, preserves list order, 
uses decimal integers without leading zeroes and one newline after each record. Canonical map/list values contain no 
insignificant whitespace. SHA-256 over canonical document bytes is the snapshot identity. SHA-256 over the canonical 
record bytes (without the trailing newline) is a record identity. Revision is not a record field. Noncanonical valid 
input can be normalized without changing its semantic values.

## Semantic registry

The complete field/type dictionary is [`registry.json`](../pixl/registry.json). That machine-readable registry and 
this specification are normative together. Unknown kinds, fields and closed-enum values fail validation; extension 
requires a new explicit version. All fields are required unless marked optional in the registry. `tg` is an optional 
list of literal tags on every kind. Map payloads are permitted only in declared fields; they cannot override core 
fields.

The bundled operational knowledge library is `knowledge/library.px`. Its K `rq` prerequisites and `rk` failure 
conditions preserve the curated source conditions. `ap` atoms are generated from source applicability phrases with 
stable SHA-256 suffixes; their exact meanings are in the hash-bound `knowledge/applicability.json` source. They are 
domain symbols, not inferred new core language rules. Source JSON is retained for curation and host interchange; the 
compiled K/S/E graph is available directly to contextual operation.

| Kind | Meaning | Operational replacement |
|---|---|---|
| A | actor and responsibility | ownership / agent role |
| P | project and active visual contract | project design envelope |
| S | source with digest and locator | reference tagging / provenance |
| R | runtime/tool dependency with exact version locator | tech stack |
| C | visual contract, dimensions, palette, view, sampling and alpha policy | art design document |
| K | applicable knowledge method, procedure, checks, research and POC references | technical knowledge |
| D | decision: `wh` who, `wt` what, `wr` where, `wy` why, `rs` research, `pc` proof | design rationale |
| T | task, ownership, dependencies, decisions, inputs/outputs, state and evidence | work plan / TODO / dependency 
graph |
| X | artifact/reference and its expected bytes, contract and metadata | asset manifest / content map |
| E | evidence receipt, outcome, mode, file hash and bound record hashes | test/review result |
| J | external job identity, request digest and observed state | tool execution memory |
| N | resumption focus, next tasks and blockers at a specific revision | contextual handoff |

References resolve within the document and must match the declared target kind. Task `dp` edges are directed from 
dependent to prerequisite and must be acyclic. Other reference cycles are legal (for example a decision and its proof) 
and closure uses visited IDs. Ready tasks are open tasks whose prerequisites are all done. Dropped prerequisites do 
not satisfy a dependency. Active/blocked/done tasks are not advertised as ready. A done task needs at least one 
current passing evidence receipt and all prerequisites done; those passing receipts must collectively bind every 
current `io` artifact record. A task cannot be done with no `io` artifact. Evidence binding 
`{rf=@id;dg=~64:<record-sha256>}` snapshots the full record, not its name. Any record change invalidates that binding. 
Passing evidence does not erase failing evidence.

Decisions require nonempty research and proof lists, and nonempty who/what/where/why content. Their current proof 
receipts must collectively bind the decision record itself and its semantic dependency closure. Proof receipts may 
report failure: a decision can reject a failed method. A K with `pc` must have current receipts binding that method 
and its semantic sources; an active/done task requires passing receipts covering that scope. K methods without 
executed proof are retained as researched knowledge, but an agent must execute an applicable POC before adoption. A 
method's recipe is not an executed proof. Agents preserve conflicting observations in separate source/evidence records 
and resolve them through a new decision rather than overwriting history.

**Semantic proof scope** traverses only `A/S/R/C/K/D/X`. It stops before workflow/project/proof kinds `P/E/T/J/N`, 
which would otherwise entangle mutable task states and proof history with the result being proved. X proof bindings 
must cover its scope, including the linked C. Link source/runtime/contract records directly when their content is part 
of the proof; a pointer through P does not bind that project's contents. K and D can point to E by ID while E binds 
their hash: the hash contains reference IDs, not recursive target hashes.

Evidence `ob=+` marks a superseded receipt. Its original outcome, file and old bindings remain preserved; outdated 
record hashes are allowed only in superseded receipts. Such receipts never count for decisions, method adoption or 
completion. Store commits forbid modifying existing E fields except one-way supersession; add a new E ID for new 
execution. Receipt metadata is a caller assertion supported by retained files. Hash validation establishes identity 
and freshness, not cryptographic attestation that a model, service or test runner told the truth.

File checking is optional for structural validation and mandatory when accepting completion in a store. With a project 
root, X/E local paths and locally addressed S bytes must exist, remain within that root after symlink resolution, and 
match their declared SHA-256. URL sources are not downloaded by validation; their digest identifies a recorded 
snapshot and does not assert current remote truth. Source `ln` ranges are inclusive, one-based and valid for the local 
source. Empty palettes mean no exact palette restriction, not zero colors. C rules apply only to linked artifacts; 
source art can intentionally choose a different contract.

### Explicit retention of imported library source revisions

A project can opt into the versioned [library source retention operation](library-retention.md). It preserves the 
exact declared local S revision in project-owned storage before replacing an installed kit, or recovers it from a 
supplied original kit. If that S locator later changes or disappears, resolution may use its explicitly retained 
locator/digest entry. The retained bytes and original line range must pass the same checks; reports identify the old 
revision actually used. This does not relax X/E file checks or substitute old bytes for a different requested digest. 
No record, evidence binding or outcome is rewritten, and no old proof is promoted to evidence about a newer 
implementation.

## Retrieval and drift

`context(focus)` includes the focus, all project roots and their transitive references, and the focus's transitive 
reference closure. For T focus it also includes associated J records and handoffs mentioning the focus. This is a 
conservative correctness baseline; it never silently truncates a required dependency or decision. If canonical packet 
bytes exceed a requested byte budget it returns a budget error with the required size. Bytes are not claimed to equal 
model tokens. Large project roots should not reference every task: tasks point to their project. Returned subsets keep 
the original revision and validate as closed documents.

`impact(id)` traverses reverse references transitively. The changed node itself is included. `ready` returns stable 
sorted task IDs. A content map is a query over X/S records and `tg`; a stack is a query over R; decisions over D. 
There are no duplicated authoritative Markdown TODO/stack state files. Source catalogs are derived indexes and can be 
rebuilt.

## State publication

A store contains `state.px`, immutable `history/<canonical-sha>.px`, and a temporary exclusive `.lock` while writing. 
Initialize once. A candidate must have exactly current revision + 1; caller must supply the current canonical hash. 
Under the lock, reload current state, compare revision/hash, validate the complete candidate and its files, 
write/fsync its history snapshot and a same-directory temporary state, then atomically replace state. The final state 
is the commit point. A crash may leave an unreferenced valid history snapshot or lock; it must not publish a partial 
document. A leftover lock fails with recovery guidance; it is never automatically broken while a writer might be 
alive. Filesystem snapshots provide local recovery, not distributed consensus or protection from a malicious 
filesystem writer. Direct manual state editing bypasses these guarantees and is unsupported for operation.

Task transitions: open -> active/blocked/dropped; active -> blocked/done/dropped; blocked -> open/active/dropped; done 
-> open; dropped -> open. Unchanged states are allowed. Existing IDs cannot disappear or change kind during commit; 
close/drop records instead. Any change to a done task or its complete reference closure requires reopening that task. 
Supersede invalidated receipts with `ob=+`, retain their original bindings, add new receipt IDs after new execution, 
and redirect D/K/T evidence references deliberately. Revision increases are mandatory even for resumption changes.

C describes raster dimensions and rendering intent. X of kind `code` or `report` associates with its production 
contract but is not interpreted as a raster image. X metadata is an opaque declared payload: the core validates wire 
values and references, not a target engine's atlas/rig schema. Domain knowledge recipes and appropriate project tools 
validate those additional contracts. A structural/file validation alone does not establish visual acceptance.

## PixelLab boundary

The host retrieves the actual connected PixelLab tool input schema. PIXL prepares a validated intent with exact tool 
name, arguments, schema digest and request digest. JSON here is the MCP provider's interchange, not PIXL syntax. 
Preparation has no remote side effect. The host calls the provider and journals the observed real ID. Generation 
starts prepared -> submitted -> running -> succeeded/failed; submitted/running may become unknown when observation is 
lost. Unknown is not permission to recreate a paid job. Recovery requires observation of the existing job; there is no 
automatic generation retry. A generation result is an artifact candidate, not proof of quality or task completion.

Durations/credits reported in research are estimates from a dated documentation snapshot. Discovered schemas take 
precedence over snapshots. PixelLab credentials remain in host secret configuration. This runtime does not impersonate 
PixelLab or invent job IDs. Its MCP server exposes local project tools only; the host connects the actual PixelLab MCP 
separately.

## Verification boundaries

The suite verifies parsing, graph invariants, writes, request validation, job transitions, artifact measurements, 
deterministic method POCs and local MCP behavior. Image inspection hashes and measures one immutable in-memory byte 
snapshot, checks every frame's dimensions and cumulative pixel limit, and labels visual/engine acceptance unverified. 
Token measurements are encoding-specific comparisons with the same semantic payload, including bootstrap overhead 
separately. They are not a controlled LLM capability or artistic-quality study. A full live PixelLab job and 
target-engine visual acceptance must be recorded with real evidence before those outcomes are asserted.

